- A 2023 longitudinal study by researchers at Drexel University tracked 382 residents living within two blocks of 29 newly established community gardens in Philadelphia over three years.
- Participants reported a 17 percent reduction in self-assessed stress levels compared to a matched control group.
- Emergency room visits for anxiety-related complaints fell by 9 percent in neighborhoods with new gardens versus comparable neighborhoods without them.
- Community garden plots in Philadelphia yield an average of 11 kilograms of produce per square meter annually.
- The city''s vacant lot inventory exceeded 40,000 parcels as of 2022.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Beyond supplying fresh produce, community gardens on former vacant lots yield measurable mental health improvements: a three-year Drexel University study found that nearby Philadelphia residents experienced a 17 percent drop in self-assessed stress, while emergency room visits for anxiety-related complaints declined 9 percent in garden neighborhoods compared to similar areas without gardens."},{"key":"B","text":"Philadelphia has over 40,000 vacant lots, and researchers at Drexel University have studied how community gardens established on some of these parcels affect nearby residents."},{"key":"C","text":"Community gardens in Philadelphia produce an average of 11 kilograms of fresh produce per square meter each year, providing a valuable source of nutrition for urban residents."},{"key":"D","text":"A 2023 Drexel University study tracked 382 residents near 29 community gardens in Philadelphia and found a 17 percent reduction in self-assessed stress, results that highlight the value of fresh produce access."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal is to argue that community gardens produce public health benefits beyond fresh produce access. The choice beginning with "Beyond supplying fresh produce" explicitly frames the argument as going past produce, then presents two specific health metrics (stress reduction and decreased emergency room visits) with comparative data, directly fulfilling the stated goal. The choice about Philadelphia''s vacant lot count and the Drexel study introduces the study context without presenting any argument or health data. The choice about produce yield per square meter focuses entirely on food production, which is the very dimension the goal asks the student to move beyond. The choice mentioning the stress reduction finding attributes the results to "the value of fresh produce access," which contradicts the goal''s requirement to 